  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}+6x}{({x}^{2}-2x)(1-2{x}^{2})}$ is $y=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}-6x}{-2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8{x}^{4}-6x}{-2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}})}{(-\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(8-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-2+\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}},$ $\\frac{4}{x},$ $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(8-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-2+\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{8-0}{-2+0+0-0} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$",
